he non‑sunny lines \\(x+y=s\\).  \nThe remaining points are those of \\(\\bigcup_{s\\in B}S_s\\); their number is  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma:=\\sum_{s\\in B}(s-1).\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line can contribute at most one point from each \\(S_s\\) (Lemma 1), so \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\cdot|B|=k^{2}\\) points of this set.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma\\le k^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Sufficiency of inequality (1)\n\nWe show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\},\n\\qquad |B|=k .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in B}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2},\n\\]\n\nso condition (1) holds. Moreover \\(s-1\\le k\\) for every \\(s\\in B\\).\n\nConstruct the required family \\(\\mathcal L\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(s\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) include the line \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   There are \\(n-k\\) such lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Introduce \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  \n   For each \\(s\\in B\\) (i.e. \\(s=2,\\dots ,k+1\\)) the set \\(S_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points.  \n   Assign these points to the first \\(s-1\\) sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\text{for }j=1,\\dots ,s-1\\text{ let }L_{j}\\text{ pass through a distinct point of }S_s .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(s-1\\le k\\), this assignment is possible, and each sunny line receives at most one point from each \\(S_s\\), respecting Lemma 1.\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered: those with sum \\(s\\notin B\\) lie on the corresponding anti‑diagonal line, and those with sum \\(s\\in B\\) lie on the sunny line to which we assigned them. The family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (inequality (1)) and the constructive sufficiency have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
