onsider the set of \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1,\\;s\\neq s_0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_0\\) covers all points of \\(D_{s_0}\\) (because \\(s_0\\) is even, the points of \\(D_{s_0}\\) with equal coordinates belong to \\(L_0\\)), and the remaining \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonal lines cover all other points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly one sunny line and \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) satisfying the conditions exists with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.  \nLet the non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) be \\(M\\) in number; then \\(M=n-k\\le n-2\\).\n\nEvery non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or an anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nA vertical or horizontal line can contain at most one point of each anti‑diagonal, whereas a line \\(D_s\\) contains **all** points whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  \nConsequently, to maximise the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines we may assume that all \\(M\\) of them are anti‑diagonal lines.  \nHence at most \\(M=n-k\\) of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal C\\); the remaining  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{\\,s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1,\\;D_s\\notin\\mathcal C\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(|B|=k\\).  \nAll points lying on the lines \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\in B\\) must therefore be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  A lower bound on the number of sunny lines needed for the set \\(B\\)\n\nFor a fixed sum \\(s\\) the set \\(D_s\\cap S_n\\) consists of exactly \\(s-1\\) lattice points (because \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\) and \\(b=s-a\\)).  \n