            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nEvery non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_c &: x=c \\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R) &&\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\nH_c &: y=c \\qquad (c\\in\\mathbb R) &&\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\nD_s &: x+y=s \\qquad (s\\in\\mathbb R) &&\\text{(anti‑diagonal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll the lines are non‑sunny, so the configuration contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep all anti‑diagonal lines except the one with sum \\(2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n+1\\,\\}\\cup\\{L\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L\\) is any sunny line passing through the unique point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \nThe line \\(L\\) covers the omitted point \\((1,1)\\); each remaining point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the appropriate anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines and one sunny line, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nOmit the three smallest anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\) and keep the others.  \nCover the omitted points with the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x ,\\\\[2pt]\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\\\[2pt]\nL_3 &: \\text{through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\\[\nL_2:\\;y= -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 ,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y= -2x+5 .\n\\]\n\nThe points of the omitted anti‑diagonals are\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),(2,1),\\;(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),\n\\]\n\nand each of them lies on one of the three lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nThe remaining anti‑diagonals \\(D_s\\;(5\\le s\\le n+1)\\) are taken as the non‑sunny lines; there are exactly \\(n-3\\) of them.  \nHence we obtain a configuration with precisely three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility results\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  No configuration with \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nLet the non‑sunny lines be \\(M=n-2\\) in number.  \nEvery non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or an anti‑diagonal.  \nIf a vertical or horizontal line is used, it can replace an anti‑diagonal without decreasing the set of covered points (each vertical/horizontal line meets each anti‑diagonal in at most one point).  \nConsequently we may assume that all \\(M\\) non‑sunny lines are anti‑diagonals; thus at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals \\(D_s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nHence at least two anti‑diagonals are omitted.  \nChoosing the two omitted anti‑diagonals to be the smallest possible, namely \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\), gives the set of uncovered points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line containing \\((1,1)\\) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(1\\); none of these three possibilities can also contain \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \nThus the three points require three distinct sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis \\(k=2\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  No configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines exists.  \nThen at most \\(n-4\\) lines are non‑sunny, so at most \\(n-4\\) anti‑diagonals can be present.  \nConsequently at least four anti‑diagonals are omitted; we may assume that the four smallest ones,\n\\(D_2,D_3,D_4,D_5\\), are omitted (omitting larger sums can only increase the difficulty).\n\nThe ten points belonging to these four anti‑diagonals are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1);\\\\\n&(1,2),(2,1);\\\\\n&(1,3),(2,2),(3,1);\\\\\n&(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line can intersect each anti‑diagonal in **at most one** point; therefore a single sunny line can cover at most four of the points in (1) (one from each anti‑diagonal).  \nConsequently at least \\(\\lceil 10/4\\rceil =3\\) sunny lines are needed just to hit one point on each of the four anti‑diagonals.\n\nThe three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) already force three distinct sunny lines (the same argument as in §2.1).  \nAfter these three lines are fixed, each of them can cover at most one further point from the remaining seven points of (1).  \nThus at most three of those seven points are already covered, leaving at least four points still uncovered.  \nSince a sunny line can meet each anti‑diagonal only once, covering the remaining four points requires at least two additional sunny lines.  \n\nHence any configuration that omits the four anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_5\\) needs **at least five** sunny lines.  \nBecause any configuration with \\(k\\ge 4\\) must omit at least four anti‑diagonals, it would require at least five sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(k\\) sunny lines are present.  \nTherefore no configuration exists for any \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3 } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
