n\nAll points of (5) lie on at least one of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nTogether with the \\(n-3\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_2\\) we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(Q\\in S_n\\) distinct from the three points, the line \\(P_1Q\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a-1},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Q=(a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the line is vertical, if \\(b=1\\) it is horizontal, and if \\(a=b\\) the slope equals \\(1\\); all three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \nHence a line through \\(P_1\\) can be sunny **only** when \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\nThe line through \\(P_2\\) and any other point of \\(S_n\\) has slope \\((2-b)/(1-a)\\); it is sunny exactly when \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\neq 2\\).  \nSimilarly, a line through \\(P_3\\) is sunny exactly when \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq 2\\).\n\nConsequently:\n\n* \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) cannot lie on the same sunny line (the line joining them has slope \\(-1\\)).  \n* No sunny line containing \\(P_2\\) can also contain \\(P_1\\) (that would be vertical).  \n* No sunny line containing \\(P_3\\) can also contain \\(P_1\\) (that would be horizontal).\n\nThus the three points (6) must belong to three **different** sunny lines.  \nTherefore every admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge 3}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \nReplacing any non‑sunny vertical or horizontal line by an anti‑diagonal never decreases the set of covered points, because a vertical (or horizontal) line meets each anti‑diagonal