lready cover the two extreme points \\((1,4)\\) and \\((4,1)\\) of \\(D_5\\); the two interior points \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,2)\\) remain uncovered.  \n   A single sunny line can meet \\(D_5\\) in at most one of these two points, so a second sunny line is insufficient.  \n   Hence \\(s=4\\) is impossible.\n\n   *The case \\(s=5\\).*  \n   The anti‑diagonal \\(D_5\\) has four points; after the two non‑sunny lines only the two interior points \\((2,3)\\) and \\((3,2)\\) are uncovered, again requiring **two** distinct sunny lines.  \n   Together with the point \\((2,2)\\) from the omitted \\(D_4\\) (which is also uncovered) we would need **three** sunny lines, contradicting the hypothesis \\(k=2\\).\n\n   In every possible situation we obtain a contradiction; therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(k\\ge 4\\) sunny lines; then there are at most \\(n-4\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   As in the previous part, the points of \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines, forcing the use of \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \n   Consequently at most \\(n-6\\) non‑sunny lines remain, while the anti‑diagonals \\(D_4,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are \\(n-3\\) in number.  \n   Hence at least **three** of these anti‑diagonals are omitted.  \n   Choose the three smallest omitted ones, namely \\(D_4,D_5,D_6\\).  \n   After the two fixed non‑sunny lines, the uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   & (2,2) &&\\text{from }D_4,\\\\\n   & (2,3),(3,2) &&\\text{from }D_5,\\\\\n   & (2,4),(3,3),(4,2) &&\\text{from }D_6 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line can meet each of the three omitted anti‑diagonals in at most one point, so a single sunny line can cover **at most three** of the seven points above.  \n   With only four sunny lines available we could cover at most \\(4\\cdot 3 =12\\) points, but the seven points already require at least **five** sunny lines 